<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 2 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Entrepreneurial Spirit</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> How to Market a Business Idea</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend detailed technical instructions. Reading: Evaluate texts for bias.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend detailed instructions on launching a business, identifying key steps in planning (e.g., "The guide recommends conducting market research before launching a product.")<br>Can evaluate bias and perspectives in articles about marketing strategies and use this information to respond to classmates' questions during a debate on how Panamanian entrepreneurs should promote their business ideas (e.g., "One article has emphasized influencer marketing as a key strategy but has ignored its cost and limited reach in local markets. I think small businesses should also consider community-based strategies that build trust.")<br>Can summarize steps in technical instructions, preparing an outline for classmates (e.g., "First, brainstorm and create a solid business idea that meets a market need. Next, draft a detailed business plan outlining goals, target audience, and financial projections. Afterward, register the business with the corresponding government entities. Finally, secure financing from banks or investors.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing images of various marketing tools (e.g., a social media ad, a local flyer, a stall at a community event, an app interface). Students are asked to quickly identify each tool and briefly discuss (in pairs) which ones they think are most effective for a new "business idea" in Panama, using vocabulary like "visibility," "customer base," or "promotional events." This activates prior knowledge and sets the context for reading about marketing strategies. (3-5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces a short paragraph (2-3 sentences) related to marketing, perhaps about launching a product. The teacher models scanning for key information (e.g., identifying the "strategy" or "target") and models the pronunciation of target words like "cost" /kɔːst/, "launch" /lɔːntʃ/, and "successful" /səkˈses.fəl/, emphasizing the long /ɔː/ vowel sound and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words. The teacher demonstrates how to identify the topic sentence for quick comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary from the previous lesson and the warm-up:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'innovation' about something new or something old?" (New)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a company wants to 'increase' its customer base, does it want more customers or fewer customers?" (More)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a printed or digital article titled "Launching Your Panamanian Business: A Step-by-Step Marketing Guide." The text is instructional (technical instructions) and describes different "strategies" for marketing, including digital and community-focused approaches. It integrates grammar points such as "If we advertise on social media, we might reach more customers" (first conditional), "Local shops have begun using social media to tell more people about their products" (present perfect for innovations), and "Next month, we'll be analyzing market trends to enhance our product line" (future continuous). The article is written for a B1.2 CEFR level, with an academic/vocational register, and incorporates the target vocabulary (e.g., "innovation," "strategy," "marketing," "customer base," "engagement," "promotional events," "visibility," "collaboration," "entrepreneur," "target").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner comprehends the main ideas and specific details in different types of texts related to entrepreneurial and marketing contexts to extract information and understand instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading):</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly (1-2 minutes) and identify its main purpose. (e.g., "What is the primary goal of this article: to entertain, to inform, or to persuade?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scanning for Details:</b> Students scan the text to identify and list three concrete steps an entrepreneur should take to "market" their business idea according to the guide.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False/Not Mentioned:</b> Students read specific statements about the marketing guide and mark them as True, False, or Not Mentioned in the text. (e.g., "The guide recommends conducting market research before launching a product." "Using an app guarantees immediate success for any business.")</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on the comprehension and application of specific grammar structures (First Conditional, Present Perfect for innovations, Future Continuous) and vocabulary related to marketing and entrepreneurship (innovation, business, strategy, marketing, customer base, automation, efficiency, costs, advances, technology, app, engagement, demand, product line, promotional events, visibility, success, collaboration, entrepreneur, target, vision, mission, objective).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Vocabulary Cloze (Controlled Reading Practice):</b> Students re-read sections of the article. They receive a worksheet with sentences from the text, but with key vocabulary words (e.g., "strategy," "innovation," "customer base," "efficiency") replaced by blanks. Students fill in the blanks using a provided word bank, ensuring they choose the word that best fits the context of the reading. (e.g., "A clear marketing ____ is crucial for a new ____.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Grammar Application (Semi-controlled Reading & Writing):</b> The teacher provides short scenarios or incomplete sentences based on the article's content or general business ideas. Students complete the sentences, applying the target grammar:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Conditional:</b> "If an entrepreneur wants to ______ (increase / reduce) their ______, they should consider investing in digital marketing." (e.g., If an entrepreneur wants to <i>increase</i> their <i>customer base</i>, they should consider investing in digital marketing.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Present Perfect for Innovations:</b> "Many local businesses ______ (implement / begin) using social media to ______ (promote / advertise) their new ______ (product line / costs)." (e.g., Many local businesses <i>have begun</i> using social media to <i>promote</i> their new <i>product line</i>.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Future Continuous:</b> "Tomorrow, our team ______ (analyze / streamline) market trends to identify new ______ (demand / advances)." (e.g., Tomorrow, our team <i>will be analyzing</i> market trends to identify new <i>demand</i>.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Bias Identification (Reading & Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides two short additional statements or paragraphs, each expressing a strong opinion on a marketing strategy (e.g., "Influencer marketing is the only way to achieve real engagement." vs. "Traditional promotional events are the true path to establishing a loyal customer base."). Students read both, discuss in pairs, and identify any potential bias or one-sided perspectives. They use phrases like, "This article has emphasized X as a key strategy but has ignored its Y."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Debate Preparation – Marketing Strategies for Panama</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are divided into small groups. Each group receives a brief case study (a short article or description) of a hypothetical Panamanian small business (e.g., a local coffee shop, a handmade craft store, a tech startup). Each case study outlines a specific marketing challenge or a proposed strategy. Students must read their group's case study carefully, focusing on understanding the context and the marketing problem or proposed solution. Their task is to evaluate the proposed strategies, identify potential strengths, weaknesses, or biases, and prepare arguments using the target vocabulary and grammar to justify or critique the strategy. The focus is on preparing for a future debate on "How Panamanian Entrepreneurs Should Promote Their Business Ideas."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Reading & Analysis:</b> Each group reads their assigned case study (150-200 words, B1.2 level) which describes a business and a proposed marketing "strategy." For example, one group might read about a business focused solely on social media "engagement," another on local "promotional events." The texts might contain subtle biases towards one approach.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Outline:</b> Within their groups, students outline the key points of their case study, identify the main marketing challenges, and evaluate the proposed solutions. They note down arguments for or against the strategy presented in their text. They are encouraged to use professional language for business ideas (e.g., "This strategy could attract more customers to our project.") and show respect for innovative suggestions (e.g., "Your idea adds an interesting approach.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Formulating Arguments (Reading for Bias/Perspective):</b> Students specifically look for information that might indicate bias in the text and formulate counter-arguments or alternative suggestions. They practice using the First Conditional ("If we implement this strategy, we might increase our visibility."), Present Perfect ("Our competitors have already launched a similar product line."), and Future Continuous ("We'll be analyzing customer feedback next week to improve our app.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Preparation for Sharing:</b> Groups prepare to briefly present their business idea and its marketing strategy, and be ready to discuss its merits or drawbacks, considering different perspectives.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a short individual written response to assess comprehension of technical instructions and ability to evaluate bias.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will complete an "Outline and Evaluate" task. Individually, students select one of the marketing guides or case studies discussed today (either the main article or their group's case study). They must:
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize three key steps or strategies for marketing a business idea from the chosen text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify one potential bias or limitation in the text's perspective or recommendations, and briefly explain why it might be biased. (e.g., "The guide emphasizes digital technology but doesn't fully discuss the costs involved for small local businesses.")</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate comprehension of main ideas and detailed instructions from the selected text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in summarizing key steps.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify and articulate a reasonable perspective or bias within the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., "strategy," "promote," "customer base," "efficiency," "costs").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new marketing strategy you learned about today that you think is most impactful for a Panamanian business?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel in identifying the main steps in an instructional text, and recognizing if a text presents a biased view?" (1 = Not confident, 5 = Very confident)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies did you find most useful today for comprehending detailed instructions and evaluating texts for bias regarding marketing ideas?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can understanding different marketing strategies and identifying bias help you make more informed decisions when considering a business venture or advising others?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully comprehend the detailed technical instructions and key steps in the marketing guide? Were they able to identify bias and different perspectives effectively in the texts provided?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What parts of the reading activities were most engaging or challenging for the students? Did the group activity effectively prepare them for evaluating marketing strategies?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for the next reading lesson, or to support students struggling with critical reading skills at B1.2?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In Lesson 3, we will shift our focus to speaking and interaction. We will use the outlines and arguments developed today (Stage 4 and 5) to engage in a structured debate or presentation about 'Effective Marketing Strategies for Panamanian Entrepreneurs,' actively using our new vocabulary and grammar to express opinions and respond to classmates' questions."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>